**Decision (One heap resource-preflight permit)** · `dec:nrz:matrix-loans-common-preflight`

Introduce one private `Heap::preflight_resources(ResourcePlan, ResourcePolicy)` entry point and require its occupation-qualified permit before any storage, mask/layout, reservation, candidate or registry growth within the common physical scope of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-symbolic-provenance-scope`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-producer-budget-scopes`). Its inventory enumerates authenticated allocation owners across legacy storage, the managed debit index, native masks/layouts and execution-owned private storage; tokens outside the heap still contribute. An owner is inventoried at its unique registry/debit entry, and every retained reference contributes only its own edge metadata. For each precision separately, `HeapLimits.elements` bounds the combined live and reserved destination elements, including companions and unused addressable capacity; backend row padding is additionally included in byte accounting. Legacy and managed descriptor occupations share the existing descriptor ceiling, native mask/layout tables keep their existing descriptor ceiling, and schema and layout-work limits keep their existing scopes. A managed reservation m, live legacy elements l and newly reserved legacy outputs o must therefore satisfy m+l+o at most H in that precision, not separate checks against H. Planned retirement cannot remove a protected owner from this inventory; only actual destruction releases its debit. The plan checks newly retained owner bytes against `OutputBytes` and this call's simultaneous peak, including headers, table replacement buffers, candidate arrays, packing/zero buffers and backend allowances, against `StagedPayloadBytes`, with the per-call scope of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-byte-scope`). Existing exact graph/provenance owners retain their own accounting scopes and are deduplicated by owner when a plan retains their references, following (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`). No mask-only subtotal or per-format legacy counter is an independent admission authority.

**Rule (Legacy entry points and the account bridge)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-legacy-preflight`

`Storage::prepare`, `binding::Reservations::prepare`, legacy evaluation/publication staging and `mask::memory::Reservation` consume that same permit. Binding validation computes its complete resource plan before `zeros` or destination slabs are allocated; the plan reserves the simultaneous legacy scratch peak for the lifetime of the exclusive `Bound`, including reserved zero destinations alongside candidate, packing and backend buffers. `ExecutionLimits` continues to bound each legacy private payload set and its existing work scope; scratch bytes also enter the common peak and cannot hide in an unregistered `Vec`. Replacements and relocation account for old owners until destruction and for new candidates before allocation. `Binding::validate_with_account` uses the supplied `ExecutionAccount` ledger for the new inventory/preparation charges and records its identity on the permit; any account-aware continuation uses that same account. Ordinary `Binding::validate` and other existing no-account calls retain their legacy Work contract and pass their existing default resource-policy ceilings to common preflight; `validate_with_resource_budget` and `validate_quantity` preserve their metadata-only caller byte ceilings under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-compatibility-scopes`). Those compatibility routes see all managed debits and cannot issue, resume or fund managed capabilities from a fresh default `Budget`; they make no cumulative managed-Work claim. A permit reserves an already counted peak, so nested helpers consume its disjoint allocation entries rather than adding the same retained bytes again through nested reservations. Account-aware scoped byte reservations restore limits on success and refusal without resetting Work. Both native formats use this bridge with unchanged limits, defaults, backend allowances and existing tariffs.

**Rule (Reserved terminal revision)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-reserved-terminal-revision`

Admission reserves one nonwrapping terminal revision successor for each pending producer as well as its prepaid authentication and cleanup entitlements. Ordinary registry mutations must leave all pending successors available; publication, cancellation or abandonment consumes exactly one producer's reservation. This makes genuine cancellation infallible after authentication even when ordinary revision growth and evaluation Work are exhausted: the owner can always close its obligation without acquiring another resource. Admission refuses before the owner escapes if that headroom is unavailable. The maximum-revision boundary is (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-terminal-revision-headroom]`), and cancellation with exhausted Work is (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-literal-work-boundary-and-prepaid-cancellation]`).

**Rule (Weak Trace residence index)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-weak-trace-index`

The execution registry keeps a weak, metadata-only index of each original `Trace`, whose counters retain no numerical owner. Inventory reads that original counter once, so an escaped unpublished factor remains visible without extending its lifetime or charging its payload again for every retained share. A backend factor's opaque allowance contributes residence bytes, never destination elements; a heap with no occupied destination elements can therefore still have live numerical residence. The weak reference keeps the counter header charged until removal, and replacement stages both the expired header and the new counter until registration drops the old weak reference. These lifetimes are controlled by (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-common-inventory-retains-unpublished-factor]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-expired-trace-replacement-counts-both-headers]`).

**Rule (Per-call bytes and heap-wide capacity)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-byte-scope`

Heap-wide destination totals are checked against `HeapLimits` across the common legacy/managed inventory, while native and descriptor occupations retain their stated table ceilings. The complete byte inventory also includes native owners and execution residence, but `OutputBytes` and `StagedPayloadBytes` are per-call ceilings, not a heap-wide resident-byte cap. They bound newly retained capacity and this call's physical staging, including old owners that it replaces until their destruction; unrelated resident payloads, masks and vacant table capacity do not become this call's bytes. A table that actually reallocates contributes its capacity increase to retained bytes and twice its old backing to scratch, so their sum covers old plus new. Reusing spare capacity or a same-call retirement vacancy adds no table delta or replacement peak. Cold affine caches enter the creating call's retained-byte plan and warm cache owners are borrowed, while definition-owned caches are excluded from later heap residence; statistical cache deduplication follows cache identity rather than equality of exact maps (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-affine-cache-retained-neighbours]`). This keeps bounded calls usable in a populated heap while refusing a combined allocation whose separately affordable parts exceed the call's ceiling. Controls are (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-managed-legacy-output-common-capacity]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-legacy-import-replacement-combined-ceiling]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-legacy-table-replacement-peak-boundaries]`).

**Rule (Metadata-only compatibility routes)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-compatibility-scopes`

`Binding::validate_with_resource_budget` and internal `validate_quantity` apply their caller's byte ceilings to execution resource metadata and check the complete physical plan with `ResourcePolicy::compatibility()` defaults. `Binding::validate_with_account` instead applies the original account's effective ceilings to the full physical plan and checks its complete destination set before numerical capture. Keeping these policies distinct preserves the meaning of existing caller budgets while allowing an account to bound all newly admitted physical owners. The metadata/physical distinction is exercised by (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-compatibility-keeps-metadata-scope]`), the quantity route by (`[MATRIX-test:integration:quantity-frequency-resource-boundaries]`), and full account admission by (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-account-refuses-before-numerical-capture]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-account-checks-complete-destination-set]`).

**Rule (Preplanned table growth policy)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-growth-policy`

Legacy slot tables and the execution registry use geometric capacity growth to amortize repeated insertion and avoid a replacement peak on every call. `growth_capacity` plans the `Vec::try_reserve` capacity policy before allocation; the admitted replacement is constructed with `try_reserve_exact` at that planned capacity and checked before moving entries. Managed admission and native tables retain exact growth so their fixed owner plans reserve only their required capacity. Sequential growth in one call keeps the earlier retained increase in that call's accounting and stages only the actual overlapping backings: growth from four to eight to sixteen rows retains twelve additional rows and reaches a twenty-four-row old-plus-new peak. Spare capacity has no replacement charge. The geometric boundary and non-growing neighbour are (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-legacy-table-replacement-peak-boundaries]`); sequential ordinary/statistical replacement is (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-sequential-growth-byte-neighbours]`).

**Rule (Temporary physical owners and compatibility timing)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-physical-staging`

Destination `Record` backings, descriptor and arena occupation keys, zero-fill outer headers, candidate owner headers and retirement keys are physical staging even though they contain metadata rather than scalar values. The complete physical plan admits those owners together with slabs, raw copies and numerical captures before those copies and captures begin; splitting descriptor and storage permits would miss their combined peak. Stack snapshots and borrowed iterators avoid temporary backings where no owner is needed. Byte admission does not move compatibility element refusals earlier: `bytes_only` projects the complete plan for early byte checks, and storage preparation consumes the same permit with the late element check; compatibility binding validation checks ordinary outputs first and the complete statistical destination set at publication. The backing and retirement controls are (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-copy-record-backing-byte-neighbours]`), (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-retirement-physical-key-neighbours]`), raw-copy refusal is (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-reading-write-raw-copy-byte-neighbours]`), and capture admission is (`[MATRIX-test:unit:loan-statistical-physical-plan-byte-neighbours]`). The thirteen/fourteen/fifteen-element publication boundary remains (`[MATRIX-test:integration:statistical-storage-limit-below-at-and-above]`); the LC-frequency staged-byte refusal prefix of 270 and successful publication Work of 3,844 remain under (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-frequency-publication-boundaries]`).

**Rule (Named symbolic and provenance scope exceptions)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-symbolic-provenance-scope`

The common physical plan deliberately leaves legacy alias and `select_reading` logical `Layout` copies, exact graph/provenance owners, schema and symbol pools, statistical/readings record registries, symbolic result declarations and logical binding registries in their established scopes. Exact entry-write selections, covers and invalidation schedules retain those scopes too; their physical numerical locations, raw copies and temporary storage records/keys belong to (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-physical-staging`). Keeping this boundary explicit avoids silently replacing exact metadata and provenance limits with a new physical-memory contract. In particular, early physical refusal does not promise that symbolic preparation allocated nothing. Separate final provenance admission is controlled by (`[MATRIX-test:integration:quantity-frequency-provenance-boundaries]`).

**Rule (Named LC and phasor producer budget exceptions)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-producer-budget-scopes`

The numerical evaluation/result owners of seven producer APIs deliberately remain outside the common plan, under their original caller `Budget` and `with_retained_payload` scopes: `publish_phasor_product` (`[MATRIX-test:unit:phasor-publication-work-boundaries-are-atomic]`); `publish_phasor_solve` (`[MATRIX-test:unit:phasor-inventory-costs-are-pinned]`); `publish_lc_spectrum` (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-root-covariance-publication-boundaries]`); `publish_lc_spectrum_reusing` (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-spectral-retained-publication-literal-boundaries]`); `publish_lc_angular_frequency` (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-angular-frequency-atomic-publication-boundaries]`); `publish_lc_frequency` (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-frequency-publication-boundaries]`); and the driven-impedance producer `publish_lc_impedance_reusing` (`[MATRIX-test:integration:lc-original-driven-publication-work-boundaries]`). Their common import permit covers the physical descriptor/arena/slab transition, not the producer's entire numerical evaluation. Preserving the separate scopes keeps their established producer figures and cumulative Work tariffs meaningful; combining those scopes requires a separate accounting change rather than inferring coverage from the shared import path.

**Decision (Occupied managed records and native shared masks)** · `dec:nrz:matrix-loans-managed-records`

Use one tagged `DescriptorRecord` in the existing descriptor slots: `Legacy(Record)` or `Managed(ManagedDescriptor)`. A managed descriptor stores its bundle root key, channel role, value/companion stamp, layout version and qualified attachment identity, never a legacy `storage::Region`. A parallel bounded group vector indexed by the root descriptor slot stores the bundle's `Reserved`, `Out`, `Published` or `Retiring` record; it shares that slot's generation, and vacant/exhausted root slots keep the existing generation rules. All channel descriptor slots and the prospective native mask/layout slots are installed as occupied `Reserved` entries under the admission borrow; the group becomes `Out` before the owner escapes, and its member reservations remain occupied under that group state. Ordinary slot searches cannot select these entries; no detached `SlotPlan` stands in for a reservation. Failed admission removes its private occupations and leaves any retained vacant table capacity counted. Legacy dense/view/alias resolution matches only `Legacy`, refusing a managed tag before making a numerical view. The native mask/layout stores use tagged reserved entries and published `Rc<MaskOwner>`/`Rc<LayoutOwner>` roots; versions keep their existing scope/key semantics. Each native root records whether it is legacy-owned or belongs to a managed bundle; automatic reclaim visits only managed-origin native roots, while legacy roots retain explicit retirement semantics. A first-slice bundle shares one immutable native identity layout among its channels and one filled-word owner among their distinct qualified attachments. The bundle retains these exact native owners, so words or maps are neither copied into a second mask store nor exposed as initialization authority through `Heap::mask`. Retirement and replacement check those owner edges before their eager native changes, resolving aliases and gather source identities to the same owners; protected changes return a pinned-use refusal before mutation. Unprotected native behavior and the exclusive lifetime of `Bound` remain intact.

**Decision (Selected readers and explicit ticket-to-pin pairing)** · `dec:nrz:matrix-loans-reader-facade`

`PublishedTile<P>` is non-cloneable and owns the initially reserved `BundlePin` plus fixed channel locators; `PublicationReceipt` contains only identities, masks and producer evidence and owns no numerical pin. Each locator identifies the checked declaration, exact bundle/channel occupation, layout and qualified attachment. `PublishedTile::reader` takes short borrows of the heap and pin, authenticates current declaration/source and target versions, and returns a `TileReader` whose selected component loads check filled coverage and charge the pin's authenticated account. A different account must acquire its own charged pin; it cannot reset this reader's continuation allowance. Its heap borrow prevents current authority from changing during that view. `BundlePin::historical_reader` is explicitly historical, borrows only the retained immutable bundle, checks the same attachment/selection bounds and supplies no authority for a new current application. Neither reader exposes a dense rectangle or a mutable view. A borrowed reader adds no use edge and cannot outlive its pin. Moving the initial pin into another owning facade transfers that edge; `retain_pin` creates a distinct charged pin with its own prepaid drop. Bare tickets, `SymbolBinding` locators and the existing cloneable `ReadingBinding`/`Group` retain no managed numerical owner, pin record or public weak-upgrade route. Managed access through a cloned locator requires explicit current `acquire_pin`, or a separately supplied matching historical pin; the legacy `ReadingBinding` dense access paths refuse managed tags. Thus cloning descriptive metadata neither extends numerical lifetime nor grants an unmetered use edge.

**Definition (Fixed local producer outcome)** · `def:nrz:matrix-loans-local-producer`

Each admitted bundle has one `ProducerRecord` inline in its group slot: the heap/account and request or execution identity, declaration and absence-scope IDs, cell capacity, receipt identity, a `Pending`, `Published(filled_mask)` or `Absent(reason)` tag, and a delivery flag. Admission reserves this fixed record and its terminal-transition entitlement; no consumer list, event queue or growing replay log is allocated. Partial publication records absence as the declared scope outside the published mask, without allocating its complement; cancellation and abandonment install the already reserved local `Absent` outcome. Publication/cancellation return their fixed receipt directly and mark this local delivery complete. An abandonment outcome remains an evidence obligation until `take_producer_outcome(ticket, account)` authenticates the exact terminal identity and original account, charges its record access, returns its reserved receipt and marks delivery complete. Repeated calls report already delivered without duplicating the transition. A terminal record remains until its payload/use and delivery obligations permit slot retirement; after reuse, stale identity is sufficient. This is local evidence delivery to the caller, not consumer-graph absence propagation or an engine flush acknowledgment under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-boundary`).

**Procedure (Resumable reconciliation and reclaim entry points)** · `alg:nrz:matrix-loans-local-scans`

`Heap::reconcile_detached(account, slot_allowance, cursor)` scans detached groups and weak pin-index records; `Heap::reclaim(account, slot_allowance, cursor)` follows with the registry-root passes of (`alg:nrz:matrix-loans-retirement`). A private cursor contains heap scope, nonwrapping registry revision, scan kind, next slot, pass endpoint and a changed-in-pass bit; it owns no numerical edge or heap borrow. Charge a slot before inspection, including a vacancy, and leave the cursor at the unvisited slot on Work refusal. Before removing a root, prepay all index/outgoing-edge traversal for that record; no partially charged removal is installed. Reconciliation authenticates the complete stored detached identity and group generations, checks expiration without weak upgrade, and uses only that record's prepaid terminal entitlement for `Abandoned`. The supplied account funds the scan, not the destroyed owner's continuation; it need not replace or replenish the original account. Successful scan mutations advance the cursor's expected revision. Any intervening admission, publication, retirement or reuse invalidates the cursor's revision and restarts its scan at the beginning, charging repeated visits; exhausted revisions refuse rather than wrap. A changed reclaim pass starts another pass, while an unchanged completed pass can report remaining pins or delivery obligations. `Heap::residence` uses these entry points and a complete inventory at one unchanged revision; an incomplete or resource-stopped scan is `ResidencePending`. This defines bounded progress without a scheduler and does not treat a dropped owner as an immediate zero-Work reclaim.

**Definition (Private first-slice owner and pin layouts)** · `def:nrz:matrix-loans-private-layouts`

Use fixed channel arrays in center, total, error order, with absent channels represented by `Option`; no per-cell object, hash table or general incidence list is allocated in this slice. `HostCore<P>` contains the sealed owner identity, `HostTileDeclaration`, an array of three optional `LoanChannel<P>` records, the native layout owner, private publication and initial-pin shells, its `CleanupReceipt`, and its sole strong liveness witness last. `LoanChannel<P>` contains one `Mat<P>`, a `Vec<u64>` of component flags of length ceil(C*k/64), a `Vec<u64>` of complete-cell flags of length ceil(C/64), and the separate payload/bitmap reservation tokens. `Liveness` contains only the owner and receipt identities. The published `BundleOwner` has a precision-tagged array of three optional channel owners, each holding its moved `Mat` and token, the moved declaration/identity, one strong native layout edge, one strong native mask edge and three optional qualified attachments inline. A single `Rc<BundleOwner>` registry root owns this complete group of separately allocated Mats; within-group target fields remain IDs. `LayoutOwner` contains the existing native axis, `Layout` and optional gather record; the first slice's affine identity layout has no map allocation. `MaskOwner` contains its layout version, fixed word vector and token. Each public pin uniquely owns an `Rc<PinRecord>` containing pin/account/bundle identities, one strong bundle edge and its cleanup entitlement; the heap has only a weak pin-index entry. No public type clones that `Rc`. Expired pin entries release their conservative header/edge debits during reconciliation; their bundle edge has already dropped. Publication shells contain optional payload fields, filled by unique access before any strong or weak reference escapes, including the initial pin's weak index entry.

**Rule (Private allocation and header byte inventory)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-private-bytes`

For h present channels, the detached numerical/bitmap payload reservation is the sum over channels of w*R + 8*ceil(C*k/64) + 8*ceil(C/64), using (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-slab-capacity`). Handback adds one prospective filled-word vector of 8*ceil(C/64) bytes, then moves it into the native `MaskOwner`; all h qualified attachments share it. Private admission allocates the layout and liveness `Rc` owners and empty bundle/initial-pin shells; handback reserves the mask shell before constructing it. Count every vector's actual capacity times its element size, and reserve before exact growth; an unexpected larger capacity is refused and remains accounted until destroyed. Count inline `Mat`, `Vec`, `Option`, identity, token, receipt, qualified-attachment and enum headers through the owning record's `size_of`, not again as independent payloads. Reserve the full detached core header while it is out and the simultaneous private shell headers during preparation. For each `Rc<T>` allocation use the aligned C-layout accounting mirror with fields strong `usize`, weak `usize`, value `T`, as in the existing private-allocation accounting; the handle fields belong to their containing records. A weak witness or pin index keeps its reference-count allocation charged until that weak reference is removed. Descriptor slots, the parallel group vector, native mask/layout slots, debit and weak-pin indexes count their full capacities, including vacant/exhausted entries and aligned tagged-record headers. Use the existing checked exact-capacity allocation boundary and replacement vectors for growth, reserving both old and new buffers until the move completes; an observed capacity mismatch never authorizes an increase to the pre-allocation ceiling. Each reserved group record holds its fixed `ProducerRecord` and prepaid terminal entitlement inline; each debit row holds the allocation key, addressable elements, bytes and receipt identity; each pin-index row holds pin identity, weak record and debit key. Shared exact declaration graphs remain separately inventoried owners, with this slice counting its retained handles/edges once. These layouts supply the byte fixtures; allocator bookkeeping beyond observable capacities and the stated backend/header allowances is not claimed as measured memory.

**Rule (Cleanup receipt inventory and fallible prefixes)** · `rule:nrz:matrix-loans-preparation-receipts`

`CleanupReceipt` is a fixed inline identity plus checked counts of allocation destruction, record closure and retaining-edge removal entitlements; reservation tokens identify the unique allocation entitlement, so moving a token never duplicates it. Its inventory names numerical slabs, both tracking vectors per present channel, filled-word staging, layout/mask/liveness/bundle/pin shells, temporary and replaced table buffers, each installed descriptor/group/debit/pin-index record, and each retained declaration, layout, mask or bundle edge. Empty vectors have no payload-allocation entitlement; their inline headers still count. An owner allocation's destruction and removal of its debit record are distinct fixed actions; grouped fields are not charged again merely because their enclosing Rust value drops. Registry-root traversal/removal stays separately charged under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-cleanup-tariffs`). Before the first fallible construction, an inline preparation guard prepays its known allocation/record/edge cleanup inventory; before any later expansion it prepays the additional inventory. A guard records the acquired prefix without allocating and closes exactly that prefix on failure, including failure of the first or last allocation. Prepaid unused entitlements are not refunded. Pending-producer installation places the terminal entitlement in its fixed record and leaves the same receipt identity and remaining destruction entitlements in the moving core, with no copied mutable allowance. Genuine cancellation consumes its prepaid authentication and terminal entitlements; abandonment uses the stored terminal entitlement once after witness expiration. Staging failure destroys only its own prepaid prefix and returns the original core. Retained vacant table capacity remains a heap owner with its debit and destruction entitlement after admission failure. Literal controls derive cleanup Work as allocation count plus record count plus non-pin retaining-edge count, plus the separately identified authentication, terminal and pin-drop entitlements; acquisition and traversal charges remain distinct. This inventory applies before success can escape, not just to the successful return path.

## Acceptance and implementation boundary · `sec:nrz:matrix-loans-evidence`

**Requirement (Ownership and repair controls)** · `req:nrz:matrix-loans-lifecycle-controls`

Exercise both binary32 and binary64 with independent expected occupation/debit transitions. Hold a live loan while an unrelated allocation completes an ordinary exclusive `Binding` execution and while another owned application evaluates; prove the out allocation remains unavailable and its full capacity counted. Test foreign heaps before slot indexing, stale generations, wrong nonce, duplicate completed tickets, generation exhaustion, swapped companions, changed target/layout/mask versions and invalid dependency witnesses. Public type controls establish that loans and writable views cannot be cloned, escaped or reconstructed from tickets; private invariant controls exercise otherwise unconstructible corrupt returns. Every refusal leaves the rightful producer, payload and reservation unchanged and returns the supplied owning capability where one exists. An initialized selected NaN, invalid radius or malformed declared companion must fail payload validation and return a repairable loan. A separate injected fallible publication-allocation or metadata-capacity failure after valid payload must do the same. Demonstrate repair followed by success, and each failure followed by cancellation with exhausted evaluation allowance. Drop a ready application, a Work-suspended application, a completed candidate and an application-repair outcome independently, also on unwind; verify the identical core/witness/receipt identity through every preceding move, output and input-pin destruction before witness expiration, conservative debits before reconciliation and exactly one authenticated `Abandoned` transition afterward. Drop a published/cancelled wrapper and repeat scans to prove that neither can become abandoned; forgetting any live detached state must keep it occupied. Include cancellation of each application state at zero evaluation Work, and interrupted/restarted scans with stale cursors and reused slots. Check no partial visibility or dependency release and exact once-only cleanup receipts under (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-lifecycle-evidence`).

**Requirement (Independent mask and companion oracles)** · `req:nrz:matrix-loans-mask-controls`

For capacities zero, one, 63, 64, 65 and 128, compare empty, sparse, partial and full coverage against independent logical sets and original-index/component tags. Include missing value components, complete values with missing companions, extra initialized orphan companions, repeated writes, explicit clearing, final-word padding and spare compacted cells. Use a value with multiple components and companions with unequal component counts, including a signed covariance block whose two target maps differ, to distinguish complete companion initialization from value initialization. For a single value channel with cells a and b, initialize only a and its mandatory companion q whose targets are both a and b; the greatest complete subset and q's published mask must both be empty. Initialize a value and its required covariance parameter U but omit U's mandatory error e_U; neither the dependent value nor U may survive, and e_U must retain U as its target when repaired. Independently enumerate the small graph's closed subsets to check these expectations, a fully initialized dependency cycle, a cycle with a missing requirement, and an initialized rootless companion cycle removed as orphan output. Use an ascending chain ending in a missing requirement to pin the repeated-pass quadratic charges, and compare the cell-local intersection to the graph result. The first-slice adapter must refuse these nonlocal/covariance declarations before allocation. Trace selected reads and scalar invocations through an instrumented accessor: zero reads/evaluations for empty demand and no hole access for sparse demand, even when hole bytes contain invalid sentinels. Compare full and masked applications against independent evaluation of exactly the selected original cells and complete companion laws; test missing reduction operands and unsupported lifting refusal. A partial kernel failure must publish only completed cells when publication is possible and retain explicit refused/pending masks. No test may validate hole safety merely because the backing zero fill made an illegal read numerically harmless.

**Requirement (Pins, movement and bounded residence controls)** · `req:nrz:matrix-loans-retention-controls`

Retain distinct binding, reader, delivery, mask-only and nested-gather pins while releasing every other edge. Assert that each prevents reclamation of its exact value/mask/companion/map closure, including the original and immediate gather parents, and that unrelated occupations retire. Exercise native mask retirement and layout replacement through every alias/gather route while a qualified attachment shares those owners. Clone locators repeatedly and verify no new numerical edge; explicitly duplicate a pin and verify its independent charged lifetime. Check that `PublishedTile` owns the initial pin, a receipt alone retains no numerical payload, borrowed readers cannot escape, and stale current access refuses while an explicitly historical selected read still succeeds. Independently check original-index movement and component tags through nested and multi-parent gathers and complete compaction; test stale parent metadata, duplicate destinations, incomplete companion movement and failed group publication. Verify that fully materialized historical associations keep identity without granting old numerical access and that a separate older-region gather still prevents release. Release the last use, observe payload/debit destruction exactly once, reuse the slot with a new generation and reject all old tickets. Include an oldest-first parent chain requiring multiple reclaim passes, terminal evidence awaiting local delivery, and a Work-exhausted reconciliation that must not report `ResidenceEmpty`. At the engine integration boundary, failed handback followed by cancellation must permit full flush only after all readers, deliveries and evidence obligations release; an outstanding borrower must keep it unfinished. This requires (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-lifecycle-evidence`) without claiming this storage slice implements the walk or flush protocol.

**Requirement (Exact resource and accounting boundaries)** · `req:nrz:matrix-loans-cost-controls`

Carry independently computed literal Work and byte boundary fixtures at sufficient allowance and its immediate insufficient neighbor, with prior cumulative usage. Cover request, scalar/word initialization, partial writes, closure scans, handback validation, late preparation failure, repair, cancellation, mask traversal, gathered movement, pin creation and repeated reclaim passes. Derive separate literals for every accepted host representation from (`def:nrz:matrix-loans-private-layouts`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-private-bytes`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-preparation-receipts`), rather than using the implementation's estimator as the oracle. Inject failure at each admission and staging allocation after cleanup prepayment, including the first and last; inspect acquired-prefix cleanup, retained table growth, spent Work and the original repairable core. Inspect payload, owner/edge metadata, actual vector capacities, backend allowance, table vacancies and simultaneous staging separately, including zero-length and row-rounding boundaries before backend allocation. With H equal to ten addressable elements per precision, reserve six managed elements and three legacy elements, then request two legacy output elements: admission must refuse before zero/candidate allocation although each isolated subtotal fits. Run that case through no-account, immutable-resource-budget and account-aware binding paths in both formats, and separately distinguish per-precision element ceilings from the combined byte peak. Include legacy scratch coexisting with output slabs and shared qualified/native masks and layouts counted once, while each pin edge counts separately. Duplicate pins must not duplicate payload charges, and a lent allocation must not disappear from unrelated operation admission. Repeat fixed-capacity admission/publication/release with bounded graph, provenance, observer and required external-history storage reported separately. Compare enabled and disabled receipt observation for identical Work and outcomes; verify zero exact per-cell Work and no descriptor/CAS calls. No acceptance result may raise a limit or overwrite an existing pin to conceal a refusal under (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-residence`), (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-accounting-coverage`).

